---
title: Matcha Basque Cheesecake
image: https://www.justonecookbook.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/Matcha-Basque-Burnt-Cheesecake-2596-I-1.jpg
source: https://www.justonecookbook.com/matcha-basque-cheesecake/
time required: 1 hour
nutrition:
  calories: 277 kcal
  fat: 22 g
  saturated fat: 12 g
  carbohydrates: 16 g
  sugar: 13 g
  protein: 5 g
  fiber: 1 g
  sodium: 138 mg
  serving size: 1 slice
cook time: 30 minutes
prep time: 30 minutes
course: Dessert
cuisine: Japanese
tags: cheesecake, matcha
servings: 8
---

Preheat a convection oven to 450ºF (230ºC) on the middle rack.

Line your #6-inch (3 inch deep) round pan{} with parchment paper.

Place @cream cheese{8%oz}(room temperature) in a large bowl. Press down on the cream cheese with the flat side of a spatula to soften it until smooth. 

Add @sugar{1/2%cup}. Combine until sugar dissolves and cream cheese is fluffy.

In a separate #bowl, whisk @eggs{2%large}(room temperature) and @egg yolk{1}(room temperature) well.

Gradually add one-third of the beaten eggs to the cream cheese mixture. Blend thoroughly until smooth and lump-free before adding the next portion.

Add another one-third of the eggs and mix until smooth and fully incorporated.

Add the remaining egg mixture and blend until smooth and fully combined. 

Ladle about two scoops of batter into a clean, #medium bowl{}.

Slowly sift @matcha{2%Tbsp}(ceremonial or culinary grade, to taste) through a fine-mesh sieve into the two scoops of batter, a little at a time. Mix with the spatula until fully incorporated and you have a thick batter.

Add one more scoop of the main batter to the matcha paste and blend well to loosen its texture.

Pour the matcha mixture back into the main batter bowl, scraping the sides clean with your spatula. Gently fold the two batters together until completely smooth and a uniform green color with no white streaks remaining.

Add @all-purpose flour{4%tsp} and @cornstarch{2%tsp} to a fine-mesh sieve. Sift the flour mixture into the batter in small increments, stirring with a spatula after each addition until smooth and lump-free.

Gradually stir in @heavy cream{220%ml}(about 1 cup minus 4 tsp, room temperature) until combined. Blend in @salt{1/8%tsp}.

Pour the batter into the prepared pan in a single, continuous stream to prevent air pockets from forming.

Tap the cake pan firmly on the counter a few times to release trapped air. Pop any remaining surface bubbles with a skewer, then gently swirl it through the batter to remove hidden air pockets.

Place the cake pan on a #baking sheet{} and transfer to the oven. Bake at 450ºF (230ºC) in a convection oven for about ~{30%minutes}, until the top is dark amber/brown and the center is still very jiggly. 

> Keep an eye on the cheesecake because every oven is different, and it can fully burn in under 5 minutes. Never extend the bake time, even if the top hasn't turned dark, as you can curdle the filling, which cannot be fixed. If the top hasn't darkened by 35 minutes, make note for next time to adjust the temperature, rack, or preheating time.

Let the cheesecake cool in the pan on a wire rack until it reaches room temperature, about ~{2-3%hours}.

> Don't let it sit on the countertop, as you need air to circulate under the cake pan.

You can serve at room temperature but it is recommended to firm up first. Cover the cake, ideally with a tea towel to avoid condensation, and place in the refrigerator for 4+ hours. When serving from chilled, let stand at room temperature for a bit first.